 is a  significant concern and as I said  earlier a lot of the employment gains  have been made in larger metropolitan  markets like Washington in New York and  San Francisco etc that's where the  housing shortages are most severe and so  the question is will new build or  inventory new supply  inventory help meet that demand because  if not we're going to see a lot of  household formation focused more on  rental housing than owner-occupied  housing and we'll see the real  owner-occupied housing growth in  subprime cities or second-level  second-tier cities where that where the  affordability factors are far more  positive for potential homebuyers mr.  Stevens the rental vacancy rate at the  end of last year it was at 7% how much  pressure is this putting on rents and  will it be enough to make renters  reconsider buying a home well the  challenge is at the end of the day is if  you can't get access to credit or if you  have trepidation about concerns about  your future stability in your job  situation you're gonna delay making that  buying decision we're seeing that  clearly today so to your point the  vacancy rate is at 7% it was at 11% a  year ago that's creating stress on  affordable rental housing that has an  impact of actually pushing long term  renters who likely aren't going to be  homebuyers into a much less affordable  lifestyle as landlords raise rents  because of the demand cycle that we're  facing so all of this is problematic  it's a tipping of the pendulum a little  too far towards the rental side and when  that imbalance gets out of whack we get  an imbalance in homeownership mister  seems we have a little less than a  minute left the Joint Center for Housing  it expects two thirds of all household  formation going forward next decade to  be minority what's the significance of  that not just for the housing market but  for the overall US economy yes so  particularly in the Latino community  which is so important to housing demand  in this country th